## Parent

[Wayfinder: make Argus stable and competitively optimized without a rewrite](https://github.com/Khamel83/argus/issues/58)

## Question

Which Argus documentation surfaces are stale, contradictory, incomplete, or unsafe when compared with the current code, generated/public metadata, deployed homelab runtime, provider profile, HTTP/MCP contracts, PostgreSQL recovery evidence, and release/deployment workflow?

Audit the Argus repository's internal and public-facing documentation, including README, LLM summaries, AGENTS/CONTEXT/CLAUDE guidance, API/MCP/provider/extraction references, configuration and examples, deploy/release/troubleshooting/operations/recovery docs, generated metadata, and historical documents that are still discoverable. Cross-check the Argus deployment and operator metadata in the homelab repository where it makes production claims.

Produce a source-backed inventory, not edits:
- canonical source for each claim;
- exact stale or contradictory statement and location;
- severity and affected audience;
- live/code evidence;
- smallest safe correction;
- whether the correction belongs in public docs, internal guidance, homelab runbooks, generated metadata, or an archive;
- explicit out-of-scope items and unresolved evidence gaps.

Do not modify documentation, close the existing operations runbook, or change production from this ticket. Separate static drift findings from live/provider checks that require credentials or spend.
